The log proves the code runs perfectly — ST7796 init sent, green fill written, no error — yet **zero** appears on the panel. Both our init and the ST7789 driver use this SPI, so the SPI bytes just aren't reaching the ST7796. I need to know *which*: is SPI comm actually dead, or is the panel powered-but-not-displaying? The decisive test is to **read a register back from the panel** — if it answers, SPI+power are fine and it's an init issue; if it returns all `0xFF`/`0x00`, SPI comm is dead.

Let me add that read. Paste this in the VM (`Ctrl-C` the server first):

```bash
export PATH=$HOME/bin:$HOME/openvela/prebuilts/gcc/linux-x86_64/xtensa-esp32s3-elf/bin:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH
F=~/openvela/nuttx/boards/xtensa/esp32s3/esp32s3-devkit/src/esp32s3_st7789.c

# insert a register-read helper + read RDDID(0x04)/RDDPM(0x0A) right after SPI freq setup
python3 - "$F" <<'PY'
import sys,re
f=sys.argv[1]; s=open(f).read()
# add read helper before st7796_init_and_fill
helper='''static void st7796_read(struct spi_dev_s *spi, uint8_t cmd, uint8_t *buf, int n)
{
  int i;
  SPI_SELECT(spi, SPIDEV_DISPLAY(0), true);
  SPI_CMDDATA(spi, SPIDEV_DISPLAY(0), true);
  SPI_SEND(spi, cmd);
  SPI_CMDDATA(spi, SPIDEV_DISPLAY(0), false);
  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) buf[i] = (uint8_t)SPI_SEND(spi, 0xFF);
  SPI_SELECT(spi, SPIDEV_DISPLAY(0), false);
}

'''
s=s.replace('static void st7796_init_and_fill', helper+'static void st7796_init_and_fill',1)
# after "SPI_SETFREQUENCY(spi, 20000000);" add reads
anchor='  SPI_SETFREQUENCY(spi, 20000000);\n'
reads=anchor+'''  {
    uint8_t rb[5];
    st7796_read(spi, 0x04, rb, 4);
    syslog(LOG_ERR, "[wslcd] RDDID(04): %02x %02x %02x %02x\\n", rb[0],rb[1],rb[2],rb[3]);
    st7796_read(spi, 0x0A, rb, 2);
    syslog(LOG_ERR, "[wslcd] RDDPM(0A): %02x %02x\\n", rb[0],rb[1]);
    st7796_read(spi, 0x09, rb, 5);
    syslog(LOG_ERR, "[wslcd] RDDST(09): %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x\\n", rb[0],rb[1],rb[2],rb[3],rb[4]);
  }
'''
s=s.replace(anchor,reads,1)
open(f,'w').write(s)
print("patched")
