The factory `config.h` is gold — and it **kills three of the four theories at once** while confirming exactly what's wrong. Straight from the code that lights this panel:

- `DISPLAY_BACKLIGHT_PIN = GPIO6` — **confirms our backlight; Claim 2 (GPIO45) is definitively wrong.**
- `MOSI=1, CLK=5, DC=3` — **exactly our SPI pins.** Good.
- `CS = NC`, **`RST = NC`** — the ST7796 has **no chip-select and no reset GPIO/expander pin.** Its CS is tied active in hardware and it resets via internal power-on.
- **There are *no* TCA9554 pins for the LCD at all.** So our `EXIO_PWR` / `EXIO_LCD_RST` writes aren't touching the display — Claim 1 was chasing a pin that doesn't drive the LCD either.

That means the *only* thing between us and a live panel is **power** — and my AXP init was **abbreviated**. I skipped the `0x80`/`0x82` DCDC setup and did a sloppy read-modify-write (`0x90` read back `0xFF`, so my "enable" was meaningless). Let me replicate the **exact** factory Pmic sequence. Paste in the VM (`Ctrl-C` server):

```bash
export PATH=$HOME/bin:$HOME/openvela/prebuilts/gcc/linux-x86_64/xtensa-esp32s3-elf/bin:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH
F=~/openvela/nuttx/boards/xtensa/esp32s3/esp32s3-devkit/src/esp32s3_st7789.c

python3 - "$F" <<'PY'
import sys
f=sys.argv[1]; s=open(f).read()
old='''  axp_wr(i2c, 0x92, (3300-500)/100);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x96, (1500-500)/100);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x97, (2800-500)/100);
  en = axp_rd(i2c, 0x90);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x90, en | 0x31);'''
new='''  axp_wr(i2c, 0x80, 0x01);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x82, (3300-1500)/100);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x90, 0x00);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x91, 0x00);
  nxsig_usleep(5000);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x92, (3300-500)/100);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x96, (1500-500)/100);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x97, (2800-500)/100);
  axp_wr(i2c, 0x90, 0x31);
  (void)en;'''
assert old in s, "anchor not found"
s=s.replace(old,new,1)
open(f,'w').write(s); print("faithful AXP init written")
PY

