The driver code is actually **correct** — our pins (1/5) take the same GPIO-matrix path as the working lckfb board (40/41), with the right signal indices (`FSPID_OUT`/`FSPICLK_OUT`), MSB-first, CS disabled. So on paper it *should* drive the panel. That means the fault is subtle, and I've been unable to prove whether the peripheral actually toggles the wire because our earlier SPI tests *also* did the panel init over the (suspect) SPI — conflating two things.

Now I can isolate it perfectly: **init the panel with bit-bang (known-good), fill it black, then fire *one* red rectangle through the SPI peripheral.** Red rectangle appears → SPI transport works. Stays black → transport is genuinely broken. Clean, single-variable test on a panel we *know* is alive:

```bash
