this is the screen......The register check didn't work because the ESP32-S3 hardware manual names the user-operation register differently. The actual configuration bit that controls the transfer execution in the ESP32-S3 architecture resides in the SPI_CMD_REG under the bit name SPI_USR.When SPI_SEND writes data to the transmitter, it sets this bit to 1. The hardware clears it back to 0 only when the internal shifting engine has completely finished emptying the serializer onto the physical pins. If your code modifies the DC pin before that bit reads 0, it breaks the timing pipeline.The Correct Hardware Drain ImplementationTo pause your thread until the hardware SPI2 engine has fully clocked out the final byte, implement the drain check by polling the SPI_USR bit:c#include "hardware/esp32s3_spi.h" // Ensure correct register layout definitions

void spi2_drain(void)
{
    /* Poll the User/Execute command bit on SPI2 (FSPI = SPI port 2) 
     * The hardware clears SPI_USR (Bit 24) when the transmission is 100% finished */
    while (getreg32(REG_SPI_CMD_BASE(2)) & (1 << 24));
}
Use code with caution.Swapping into the Windowed putarea PathIntegrate this function into your custom putarea wrapper right around your command boundaries to isolate the timing issue:c// Example window command sequence integration
void board_lcd_putarea(uint16_t start_x, uint16_t end_x, uint16_t start_y, uint16_t end_y, uint16_t *pixels)
{
    /* 1. Send Column Setup Command */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC to Command Mode
    SPI_SEND(spi, 0x2A);
    spi2_drain();               // WAITING: Ensure command clocked out before DC changes

    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC to Data Mode
    SPI_SEND(spi, (start_x >> 8) & 0xFF);
    SPI_SEND(spi, start_x & 0xFF);
    SPI_SEND(spi, (end_x >> 8) & 0xFF);
    SPI_SEND(spi, end_x & 0xFF);
    spi2_drain();               // WAITING: Ensure window parameters clear before next DC shift

    /* 2. Send Row Setup Command */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC back to Command Mode
    SPI_SEND(spi, 0x2B);
    spi2_drain();               // WAITING

