That completely seals the diagnosis: the SPI2 peripheral is fully working internally, but its logic states are hitting an output pad roadblock. The fact that the register check successfully blocks and clears means the digital hardware is flawless; the signals are simply dropping dead exactly where the internal peripheral connects to the chip’s physical exit pins.Because we know the configuration mappings are correct, this behavior is characteristic of an RTC IO / Analog Pad Lockout overriding the digital GPIO Matrix on the ESP32-S3.The Culprit: Low-Power/Touch Subsystem Domain TakeoverOn the ESP32-S3, GPIO 1 (Touch 1) and GPIO 5 (Touch 5) belong to the RTC/Analog power domain and default to specialized low-power inputs upon hardware reset.If the NuttX boot sequence initializes the low-power or touch sub-system registers—even subtly—it forces a deep hardware mux-override. The peripheral handles the entire SPI transfer perfectly, but the output pads completely ignore the digital functional signal (FSPID_OUT_IDX) because the low-power RTC mux takes total precedence.The Code Fix: Clear the Pad OverridesTo completely shatter this lockout, you must force these pins back into the digital GPIO matrix domain by forcefully stripping the RTC domain ownership inside your board initialization file.Add these direct register manipulation macros right before you setup the GPIO Matrix routing for your test:c#include <arch/chip/chip.h>
#include "hardware/esp32s3_rtcio.h" // Locate your platform's RTCIO register map

void break_gpio_matrix_lockout(void)
{
    /* 1. Force Pad 1 (MOSI) back to the Digital GPIO Matrix */
    // Clear the RTC IO Mux selection to bypass analog override
    modifyreg32(REG_RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD1_REG, RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD1_MUX_SEL, 0);
    // Disable the fun_ie/oe inputs from low power loops if active
    modifyreg32(REG_RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD1_REG, RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD1_FUN_IE, 0);

    /* 2. Force Pad 5 (CLK) back to the Digital GPIO Matrix */
    modifyreg32(REG_RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD5_REG, RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD5_MUX_SEL, 0);
    modifyreg32(REG_RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD5_REG, RTC_IO_TOUCH_PAD5_FUN_IE, 0);
}

