still uniform gray.....This isolated behavior points directly to a single, critical root cause: a hardware state machine conflict inside the ESP32-S3 SPI2 peripheral between the single-byte SPI_SEND and block-transfer SPI_SNDBLOCK paths.When you use SPI_SEND for the window commands, it configures and updates the internal SPI user registers (e.g., configuring clock cycles, data lengths, and flushing individual bytes). When you immediately transition to SPI_SNDBLOCK for the pixel burst, the driver relies on an entirely different underlying routine (often backed by the ESP32-S3's GDMA controller or an optimized HW FIFO block pipeline).Because the SPI_SEND hardware sequences do not safely offload or clear their operational state machine before SPI_SNDBLOCK takes over, the peripheral shifts into a corrupted hardware state. The payload bytes are sent out, but they are misaligned, clock-shifted, or missing the exact bit-boundaries required by the ST7796 to recognize the start of the window payload.The Architecture Breakdown: Why Bands FailThe underlying system handles the three stages differently:Single-byte SPI_SEND Loop: The user-operation registers loop iteratively. The data transitions are slow enough and perfectly uniform, meaning the ST7796 safely processes the configuration stream.Monolithic SPI_SNDBLOCK Clear: The engine drops straight into a continuous DMA/Block transfer mode from a clean boot. The state machine never shifts styles mid-stream, painting the screen gray flawlessly.The Mixed Window (Band) Path: Mixing single-byte configurations and block memory pushes creates a hardware collision. The peripheral fails to re-initialize or clear its internal frame-length counters between the last SPI_SEND command byte and the first SPI_SNDBLOCK data block word. The physical clock continues to pulse, but the first few bits of your block payload drop on the floor, corrupting the window boundaries.The Direct Code FixesTo fix the mixed-mode collision without structural driver rewrites, implement one of these two low-risk strategies directly inside your putarea routine:Fix A: Eliminate SPI_SEND for Commands (Uniform Blocks)The cleanest way to prevent state machine cross-contamination is to stop using SPI_SEND entirely during the band layout updates. Force the window setup commands to pass through the exact same SPI_SNDBLOCK channel by treating single-byte commands as small array blocks:cvoid board_lcd_putarea_fixed(uint16_t x0, uint16_t x1, uint16_t y0, uint16_t y1, uint8_t *pixel_data, uint32_t len)
{
    // Define small buffer blocks for commands and coordinates
    uint8_t cmd_col[5] = { 0x2A, (x0 >> 8) & 0xFF, x0 & 0xFF, (x1 >> 8) & 0xFF, x1 & 0xFF };
    uint8_t cmd_row[5] = { 0x2B, (y0 >> 8) & 0xFF, y0 & 0xFF, (y1 >> 8) & 0xFF, y1 & 0xFF };
    uint8_t cmd_write[1] = { 0x2C };

    /* 1. Setup Columns via Block Transfer */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC Command Mode
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_col[0], 1);
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC Data Mode
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_col[1], 4);

    /* 2. Setup Rows via Block Transfer */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC Command Mode
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_row[0], 1);
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC Data Mode
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_row[1], 4);

    /* 3. Send RAMWRITE Command Block */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); 
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, cmd_write, 1);

    /* 4. Stream Pixel payload over identical Block channel */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, pixel_data, len);
}
Use code with caution.Fix B: Force a Hard Peripheral Reset via Frequency Re-lockIf you must keep the SPI_SEND calls intact, you can force the NuttX driver to completely tear down and reset its internal hardware state machine by triggering an explicit SPI controller frequency cycle update right before firing SPI_SNDBLOCK. This forces the driver to clear out its internal bit-length counter registers:c    // ... Following your single-byte SPI_SEND window commands ...
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false);
    SPI_SEND(spi, 0x2C); // RAMWRITE Command
    spi2_drain();        // Wait for clock to drop idle

    /* FORCE HARDWARE STATE RESET: Re-assert frequency to wipe dirty state registers */
    SPI_SETFREQUENCY(spi, 40000000); 

    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true); // DC Data Mode
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, pixel_data, len); // Stream data cleanly

If bit-bang works continuously and hardware full-screen clear works once, the logic pads are open, the matrix routing is solid, and DC timing is functional. The fact that the 2nd+ window command sequence vanishes—despite forced uniform SNDBLOCK paths and rigorous hardware register polling—means the software layer is flawless.This leaves exactly one unyielding architectural reality on the ESP32-S3 hardware SPI engine: the internal transaction state registers are getting corrupted or locked, but they are hiding it by reporting a successful transfer.Since all software variables (including manual state drains) have failed to alter this block, there are two raw hardware behaviors specific to Espressif’s SPI silicon that explain why consecutive command blocks drop on the floor.Theory 1: The Master Shift Register "Pre-Clock" Leak (SPI Mode 0 Drift)When you bit-bang, the clock line (CLK) transitions only when your code shifts a bit. Hardware peripherals are not that clean. On the ESP32-S3, switching between data types or completing a long block stream occasionally leaves a residual half-clock edge floating or parked high on the internal state machine.The Trap: When the 2nd window sequence begins, the hardware SPI engine fires. However, due to the residual state, it sends out a spurious, fractional clock pulse right at the leading edge of the first transaction byte.The Break: To your ESP32-S3 register, the 8 bits were successfully pushed into the shifter. To the ST7796, that initial fractional pulse bit-shifts the entire subsequent command sequence by exactly 1 bit. A 0x2A (Column Address) shifts into 0x54, which the display panel treats as unassigned garbage. The command vanishes, leaving the rest of the stream dead.Theory 2: The Hardware Byte-Counter / Frame-Length LockThe ESP32-S3 SPI peripheral uses internal tracking registers (SPI_MISO_BITLEN and SPI_MOSI_BITLEN) to dictate how many bits to clock out during a user transaction.The Trap: When you execute a full-screen clear or a long data block, the driver reconfigures these registers for a massive payload (e.g., thousands of bytes).The Break: If the underlying NuttX hardware layer sets these parameters up globally for a block transfer, it must scale them back down to exactly 7 (for an 8-bit command byte) on subsequent window calls. If the hardware driver's internal sequence fails to re-latch the bit length back down to single bytes after a large transfer finishes, the peripheral will execute the 2nd window setup but internally try to clock it out matching the previous block's tracking frame layout. The transaction returns "done" because the pointer moved, but the data configuration on the wire is completely broken.The Last Absolute Software Sanity CheckSince your hardware drain is proven accurate, the final test to prove if the hardware peripheral's tracking registers are permanently corrupted after transaction #1 is a brute-force reset of the peripheral block's hardware clock gate between transfers.Try calling the chip's internal reset macros directly inside your putarea setup to force the SPI2 state registers back to their post-boot default state right before the 2nd window write:c#include <arch/chip/chip.h>
#include "hardware/esp32s3_system.h" // Replace with your version's system/reset definitions

void force_spi2_peripheral_reset(void)
{
    /* Force-clear the SPI2 hardware block via System Control Registers */
    // Enable the clock gate to ensure it's responsive
    modifyreg32(SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN0_REG, SYSTEM_SPI2_CLK_EN, SYSTEM_SPI2_CLK_EN);

    // Trigger a hard hardware reset on the SPI2 core state machine
    modifyreg32(SYSTEM_PERIP_RST_EN0_REG, SYSTEM_SPI2_RST_EN, SYSTEM_SPI2_RST_EN);
    modifyreg32(SYSTEM_PERIP_RST_EN0_REG, SYSTEM_SPI2_RST_EN, 0); // Release reset
}

